        "example": "Imagine a shared household shopping list on a kitchen whiteboard. The whiteboard says: 'Milk: 2 cartons'. Parent Alice looks at the board, decides the family needs one carton, goes to the store, buys one carton, and changes the board to 'Milk: 1 carton'. While Alice was at the store, Parent Bob looked at the original board ('Milk: 2 cartons'), went to a different store, bought one carton, came home, and wrote 'Milk: 1 carton'. The family bought two cartons of milk, but the board reflects only one purchase.",
