        "part": 4,
        "title": "High-Throughput Ticket Reservation Engine: Queue Partitioning, Dynamic Expiry & Advisory Locks",
        "say": [
          "Having architected atomic inventory allocation, the engineering team constructs the complete production-grade High-Throughput Ticket Reservation Engine. In enterprise ticketing and banking platforms, inventory reservation is not an instantaneous binary state; it is a multi-stage transactional lifecycle comprising reservation, checkout holding, payment settlement, and final ticket issuance.",
          "To prevent held reservations from blocking permanent inventory when users abandon checkout carts, the reservation engine implements Dynamic Expiration Leases. When a user claims an available seat using SKIP LOCKED, the engine transitions the seat status to 'HELD', records a reserved_until timestamp (set to five minutes in the future), and records the customer's unique session identifier.",
          "If the customer successfully completes credit card payment within five minutes, the payment webhook transitions the status to 'PURCHASED', issuing the immutable ticket. If the customer closes their browser or abandons the cart, the reservation expires naturally. A high-speed background janitor cron query scans for expired holds: UPDATE seat_inventory SET status = 'AVAILABLE', user_id = NULL, reserved_until = NULL WHERE status = 'HELD' AND reserved_until < NOW(), instantly recycling seats back to waiting shoppers.",
          "To ensure that background janitor recycling executes with minimal overhead, reliability engineers deploy a partial index: CREATE INDEX idx_held_expired ON seat_inventory (reserved_until) WHERE status = 'HELD'. Because only held seats are indexed, the background query executes an index scan over a tiny fraction of the table, recycling thousands of expired carts in single-digit milliseconds without acquiring table-level locks.",
          "To scale reservation throughput beyond single-table limits, the architecture introduces Queue Partitioning. Inventory is divided into discrete Partition Buckets based on venue sections or hash ranges (partition_bucket equals mod(seat_id, 16)). Each partition bucket is assigned to an independent worker node. Workers operate on separate database partitions, eliminating B-tree root page lock contention and achieving horizontal scaling across CPU cores.",
          "Furthermore, the reservation engine deploys PostgreSQL Advisory Locks to enforce strict per-customer rate limits and prevent double-spending: before a customer can initiate a checkout, the worker acquires a transaction-level advisory lock on the customer's hashed user ID: SELECT pg_try_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext('user_reservation:' || customer_id)). If the customer rapidly clicks 'Reserve' five times across multiple browser tabs, only the first request acquires the advisory lock; the four duplicate requests fail the trial lock instantly and are rejected with HTTP 429 Too Many Requests.",
          "Crucially, the entire reservation transaction enforces Global Canonical Resource Ordering. If a customer reserves two adjacent seats (seat twelve and seat thirteen), the SQL query sorts the seat IDs in strict ascending order before executing SELECT FOR UPDATE: SELECT seat_id FROM seat_inventory WHERE seat_id IN (12, 13) ORDER BY seat_id FOR UPDATE. This mathematical ordering guarantees that concurrent transactions can never form circular lock dependencies, completely eliminating deadlock aborts (SQLSTATE 40P01).",
          "In addition, the database connection pool is optimized with PgBouncer in transaction pooling mode: server_reset_query is configured, and statement_timeout is set to two seconds. Stalled or rogue queries are automatically aborted by the PostgreSQL kernel, ensuring that connection slots are returned to the pool within milliseconds.",
          "To protect against network retries and duplicate user submissions, the reservation pipeline mandates the use of Cryptographic Idempotency Keys. Every client checkout request includes a UUID idempotency key generated by the front-end client. The payment settlement transaction attempts to insert the idempotency key into a payment_idempotency table with an immutable UNIQUE constraint. If a duplicate webhook or impatient customer submits a repeat request, the database rejects the duplicate with an immediate unique violation (SQLSTATE 23505), completely preventing duplicate charges, inventory desynchronization, and double-spend settlements.",
          "By uniting dynamic expiration leases, partitioned queue buckets, advisory lock singletons, and canonical lock ordering, software architects deliver a fortress-grade reservation engine that thrives under maximum concurrency."
        ],
